# ── The AGENTS.md this repo DOES track, stated explicitly (t/2080) ───────────
# Rule: an AGENTS.md is tracked HERE iff a public-repo consumer needs it WITHOUT
# the private Orca overlay. Everything else lives in the overlay (.orca-git).
#
# Before these lines, tracking happened only via `git add -f`, so the set was
# implied by commit history rather than declared — and the overlay's own
# whitelist (`!**/AGENTS.md`, unconditional) had no way to know what this repo
# had claimed. Every `add -f` silently produced a file tracked in BOTH repos,
# free to diverge; root AGENTS.md did exactly that. Declaring the set here and
# re-excluding the same two paths in `.orca-gitignore` makes the two tracking
# sets disjoint by construction.
#
# Adding a path here publishes that file. Do it only with the matching
# `.orca-gitignore` re-exclusion, or you re-create the double-track.
# `.githooks/agent-file-owner.sh --audit` fails if the two lists drift apart.
!/AGENTS.md
!/operations/devops/azure/AGENTS.md

